/*! @class doc_isolation_levels
Three isolation models are supported in WiredTiger, from weaker to stronger:

- <code>read-uncommitted</code>:
Transactions can see changes made by other transactions before those
transactions are committed.  Dirty reads, non-repeatable reads and
phantoms are possible.

- <code>read-committed</code>:
Transactions cannot see changes made by other transactions before those
transactions are committed.  Dirty reads are not possible;
non-repeatable reads and phantoms are possible.  Committed changes from
concurrent transactions become visible periodically during the lifecycle
of the transaction.

- <code>snapshot</code>:
Transactions read the versions of records committed before the transaction
started.  Dirty reads and non-repeatable reads are not possible; phantoms
are possible.
Snapshot isolation is the default isolation level, and all updates must be
done using snapshot isolation.

<!-- FIXME-WT-3983: WT-3983 wants this to be clear on the distinction between
phantom reads and write skew, which probably needs examples of both. -->
*/

/*! @page explain_isolation Tutorial: isolation levels

Traditionally, because strict adherence to the ACID semantics can be
expensive, database systems provide a range of isolation models,
trading off weaker consistency/isolation guarantees for performance.

@copydoc doc_isolation_levels

@section explain_isolation_write_skew Write skew

Under snapshot isolation, each transaction reads a consistent snapshot
that excludes changes made by concurrent transactions. <i>Write skew</i>
is an anomaly in which concurrent transactions each make decisions using data
that the other transaction changes, but update different records. Both may
commit because their writes do not conflict, even when their combined changes
violate an application rule. The resulting outcome may be impossible if the
transactions were executed one after another. <i>Serializable</i> isolation
guarantees behavior equivalent to some serial ordering of the transactions,
but WiredTiger does not support that isolation level.

For example, an application stores reservation counts for groups A and B in
two separate records. Each transaction attempts to add 15 reservations to its
group after checking that the combined total would not exceed 100. The diagram
below shows both transactions running under snapshot isolation, with time
flowing downward.

@plantuml_start{snapshot_write_skew.png }
@startuml{snapshot_write_skew.png}
hide footbox
skinparam monochrome true
skinparam shadowing false
title Write skew under snapshot isolation

participant "T1: reserve for group A" as T1
participant "T2: reserve for group B" as T2

note over T1, T2
  Initially: A = 40, B = 40
  Application rule: A + B <= 100
end note

T1 -> T1: Begin transaction
T2 -> T2: Begin transaction
T1 -> T1: Read A = 40, B = 40
T2 -> T2: Read A = 40, B = 40
T1 -> T1: Check 80 + 15 <= 100: passes
T2 -> T2: Check 80 + 15 <= 100: passes
T1 -> T1: Write A = 55
T2 -> T2: Write B = 55
T1 -> T1: Commit
T2 -> T2: Commit

note over T1, T2
  Final state: A = 55, B = 55
  Total = 110: application rule violated
end note
@enduml
@plantuml_end

Both transactions see the original total of 80 in their snapshots, so each
check passes: adding 15 would give a total of 95. T1 updates only A and T2
updates only B, so their writes do not conflict and both may commit. The final
total is 110, violating the application rule even though each transaction's
check passed.

If these transactions ran one after another, the first would increase the
total to 95. The second would then read that total and reject its addition,
because 95 + 15 exceeds 100. Neither serial ordering would produce a total
of 110. This difference illustrates why snapshot isolation does not guarantee
serializable behavior.

*/
